    modelAnswer:
      "Hallucination is when an LLM generates text that reads as fluent and confident -- stylistically indistinguishable from a correct answer -- but is factually wrong or entirely fabricated: a citation to a paper that doesn't exist, a method that was never part of a library's real API, a plausible-sounding but false historical fact. It happens because an LLM is fundamentally trained to predict the next most statistically plausible token given its context, based on patterns learned from training data -- it has no separate, built-in mechanism that checks 'is this actually true' versus 'does this sound like a reasonable continuation'. A confidently wrong answer is produced via the exact same generation process as a confidently correct one; there's no internal 'I don't actually know this' signal it's reliably consulting before generating fluent-sounding text. Simply instructing the model 'don't make things up' in the prompt is an incomplete fix because it can only influence the model's STYLE or tendency (e.g. nudging it to hedge or say 'I'm not sure' more often in cases that are genuinely ambiguous to it) -- it cannot give the model actual knowledge it doesn't have, and it cannot create a true fact-checking capability where none exists; the model still has no ground-truth signal, it can just be prompted to express UNCERTAINTY more often, which is a real but limited improvement, not a fix for the underlying cause. More effective complementary approaches: grounding the model's answer in actually-retrieved real documents (RAG), so it has concrete source material to draw from rather than relying purely on parametric memory; adding a separate verification step that checks specific factual claims in the output against trusted sources before showing them to a user; and designing the overall system (UI, workflow) to surface uncertainty and encourage verification, rather than architecting around an assumption that a single prompt instruction will make hallucination reliably go away.",
