      "Traditional ML classifier evaluation is clean because there's a fixed set of labels and a clear ground truth to compare against -- accuracy, precision, recall all have unambiguous definitions. LLM output evaluation is harder because the output is open-ended free text: there's often no single 'correct answer' string to exact-match against (many different phrasings could all be equally 'correct'), and what counts as 'good' is genuinely multi-dimensional and partly subjective -- helpfulness, factual correctness, appropriate tone, conciseness, and policy compliance (e.g. not making promises the company can't keep) might all matter and can trade off against each other. A real evaluation process starts with building a representative, curated evaluation SET: a fixed collection of realistic input scenarios, ideally including known-hard or historically-problematic cases pulled from real past support tickets, used CONSISTENTLY across every model/prompt version being compared -- so you're making apples-to-apples comparisons over time rather than ad-hoc spot-checking different inputs each time. On top of that fixed set, use a combination of evaluation methods, since no single one is sufficient alone: automated checks for the things that genuinely ARE mechanically checkable (does the response include a required disclaimer, does it stay under a length limit, does it avoid specific forbidden claims or PII leakage); LLM-as-judge scoring, where a separate model call rates each response against an explicit rubric, which scales well but has its own known limitations (it inherits biases and blind spots, and isn't a perfect proxy for real human judgment) so its scores should be treated as a useful signal, not ground truth; and genuine human review on a sample, especially concentrated on the highest-stakes or most ambiguous cases, as the actual ground-truth check that the other methods are approximations of. Finally, because no offline evaluation set can perfectly capture the full diversity and ongoing drift of real production traffic, the process shouldn't stop at offline evaluation -- roll the new version out gradually (a canary or A/B test against a small percentage of real traffic) and monitor actual downstream business/user metrics (ticket resolution rate, escalation-to-human rate, explicit user satisfaction feedback) before committing to a full rollout, treating offline eval as a necessary gate, not a sufficient one.",
